/** Classic-album criticism and artist pieces. Public record only; no invented quotes. */
module.exports = {
  untrue: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Burial's second album, Hyperdub, 5 November 2007: garage slowed down until the walk home is the song.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Untrue</em> is Burial’s second album, released on Hyperdub on 5 November 2007, a year after the self-titled debut that had already made a faceless South London producer the most argued-about new name in British dance music. William Bevan’s identity was not public yet. The records arrived as sleeves, a few interviews conducted by email, and a sound that listeners tried to file under dubstep because that was the word the year had available. The filing was never quite right, which is why the album is still the one to hand someone who thinks dubstep means a festival drop.`,
      `What a non-specialist needs is a plain description of the materials. The rhythms come from UK garage, the 2-step pattern that dominated British clubs and pirate radio at the end of the 1990s: a kick that refuses a four-on-the-floor march, a shuffle, a skip. Burial drags that pattern down in tempo and leaves the crackle of vinyl, or of a cheap recording, sitting on top of it as if the record were already used. The voices are samples, pitched and cut until they sound remembered rather than performed. Nobody is credited as a singer in the way a pop album credits a singer. The voice is a material, like the hiss.`,
      `The tracklist is the argument. “Archangel,” “Near Dark,” “Ghost Hardware,” “Etched Headplate,” “In McDonalds,” “Etched Headplate”’s neighbors “Shell of Light” and “Raver,” the title track. “In McDonalds” is the title that gives the geography away without a postcard. This is not nightclub music in the sense of a room with a lighting rig. It is the music of the time after the room, named for ordinary places, built from a dance rhythm that has lost the crowd and kept the drum. People called the crackle atmosphere. Atmosphere is what you say when you do not want to admit that the distortion is composition. He chose it. It is not the residue of a bad transfer. Or if it is residue, it is residue he refused to clean, which is the same decision.`,
      `The debut had already established the palette. <em>Untrue</em> is the one that made the palette feel like a world rather than a single idea repeated. Songs arrive with something closer to a chorus — a vocal phrase that returns — without ever becoming songs you could hand to a conventional vocalist. That halfway state is the record’s invention. Garage had been full of vocal tracks, diva edits, hearts on sleeves. Dubstep, in the form that was about to go global, was about to become largely instrumental and very loud. Burial stood in the door between them and did not pick a side. The emotional register is grief, or homesickness, and it is not vague. “Raver” is a title with a thesis: the person who went out is now the subject, past tense built into the noun.`,
      `Critical reputation did this album a partial disservice by turning it into a shortcut. For a few years, <em>Untrue</em> was the record writers used to prove that electronic music could be melancholy and serious, as if melancholy were a promotion out of the dance. The drums say otherwise. They are dance drums, from a specific British club lineage, played at a speed you can still move to in a kitchen. The seriousness is not that the album escaped the club. It is that it took the club’s rhythm home and refused to brighten it up for company. Hyperdub, Steve Goodman’s label, was the right door for that refusal. The label’s other releases made a context: this was a scene, not a solitary genius myth, even if the sleeves encouraged the myth by withholding a face.`,
      `Heard in 2026, the album’s risk is nostalgia of a second order. Listeners now approach it as a classic, which means they listen for the crackle they already love and miss the severity of the editing. The vocals are not there to be sad on your behalf. They are chopped until a feeling becomes a rhythm. That is closer to hip-hop’s treatment of a sample than to a singer-songwriter’s treatment of a confession, and it is the part that still has work to do. If you want the rain-on-the-glass version, it exists, and it is smaller than the record. The record is a garage album that accepted daylight was not coming and built the songs anyway. South London is in the titles and in the interviews he eventually gave. You do not need our coast to understand it. You need a bus that has stopped running and a drum pattern that has not.`
    ]
  },
  homogenic: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Björk's third album, 1997, with Mark Bell and a string octet: beats and orchestra as the same weather.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Homogenic</em> is Björk’s third studio album, released in September 1997, after <em>Debut</em> (1993) and <em>Post</em> (1995). She made it with Mark Bell, of the Sheffield group LFO, and with string arrangements played by the Icelandic String Octet. The combination is the entire concept and it is announced in the music rather than in a manifesto: electronic rhythm and a small orchestra occupying the same bar, neither of them a guest. One Little Indian released it. The singles a general listener is most likely to have met — “Jóga,” “Bachelorette,” “Hunter,” “All Is Full of Love” — are doors into an album that is stricter than the singles’ fame suggests.`,
      `The strictness is the sound-world. Where <em>Post</em> had been willing to change costume every track, from big-band brass to industrial thud, <em>Homogenic</em> stays inside one climate. Beats are bright, hard, and close. Strings are not the pretty layer added to make the electronics respectable. They carry melody and they carry dread. “Jóga” is the clearest demonstration: a landscape song, written for a friend, in which the percussion keeps erupting under a string line that would have been enough for a film cue. Björk’s voice sits forward, Icelandic accent intact, words about emotional landscapes that the arrangement refuses to illustrate gently. Gentleness would have made it a nature documentary. The beats make it physical.`,
      `“Bachelorette” is the album’s most direct piece of theatre, a song that walks in already certain of its ending, strings marching, vocal in full command. “Unravel” is the opposite pressure, a ballad about love as a thread that comes undone, and it is the track that later singers kept returning to because the melody is generous and the arrangement leaves room. “All Is Full of Love,” which closed the album and later became a single with Chris Cunningham’s famous video of two machines kissing, is the afterimage: a lyric of total affection set to a rhythm that sounds like a factory trying to learn tenderness. “Pluto” is the other pole, a blast of speed, voice shredded by the processing. An album that can hold “Unravel” and “Pluto” without sounding like a compilation is an album with a spine.`,
      `Bell’s role matters to anyone trying to hear this as electronic music rather than as a pop star’s orchestral moment. LFO had come out of the early British rave years with a sense of bass as structure. On <em>Homogenic</em> that sense keeps the strings from floating off into prestige. The low end is blunt. The album was partly made away from London, after a frightening episode in which a fan sent a letter bomb; the move is part of the public story of the record and it matches the music’s desire for a smaller, harder set of materials. Iceland, in the sound, is not a postcard of geysers. It is the decision to use Icelandic players and to stop collecting genres.`,
      `The reputation that followed has sometimes stranded the album in “art” and away from the dance floor it actually understands. These are body rhythms. “Alarm Call” and “Hunter” are as close to club music as a song with this much melodic information can get without giving up the song. The critical mistake is to treat the orchestra as the serious part and the beats as the modern part. They are one idea: a human voice and an acoustic ensemble pushed through the same electronic pressure. That idea turned out to be one of the durable ones in the decades since, audible whenever a producer puts a string line against a drum machine and hopes the collision produces feeling rather than a trailer. Björk and Bell got there with the collision already finished, not hoped for.`,
      `For this magazine the album is a reference, not a mood board. Club Copy’s own pages keep separating melody from rhythm and then trying to put them back in one credit: Riscape’s piano against house time, LT Drifta’s jazz titles against beat lengths. <em>Homogenic</em> is the popular record that showed the reunion could be severe. It does not sound like those records. It shows why a listener who cares about one might care about the other. Put “Jóga” on for the landscape. Stay for “Pluto,” or you have only heard the half that flatters you.`
    ]
  },
  donuts: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "J Dilla's Donuts, Stones Throw, 7 February 2006: thirty-one short instrumentals, released three days before he died.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Donuts</em> came out on Stones Throw on 7 February 2006. James Dewitt Yancey, J Dilla, died on 10 February. The album is thirty-one instrumental tracks, most of them very short, made largely from samples. The proximity of the release to his death has dominated the listening ever since, and it should not be sanded off. It also should not be the only way in. A record that is only a funeral is a record you are afraid to learn from. <em>Donuts</em> is a manual.`,
      `The manual is about time. Dilla’s drums, on this album and on the productions that made his name with Slum Village, Erykah Badu, The Pharcyde, and a long list of others, sit behind and ahead of the grid in a way that became a cliché to describe and remained difficult to do. Hats are late. Snares are early or drunk or both. The loop does not resolve where a sequencer wants it to resolve. On <em>Donuts</em> the loops are short enough that you hear the decision every few bars: a soul phrase, a rock riff, a spoken scrap, flipped and left alone just long enough to register as a thought. “Workinonit,” “Time: The Donut of the Heart,” “Lightworks,” “Two Can Win,” “Last Donut of the Night,” “Welcome to the Show” — the titles are jokes and they are also a sequence. The album opens at the end, in the famous structural wink that makes the last track the introduction. A circle, a donut. The sleeve’s photograph, his face, slightly smiling, is part of the same tone. The music is playful at the level of the edit and serious at the level of the touch.`,
      `Stones Throw’s account, and his mother Maureen Yancey’s public account, place a lot of the album’s finishing in the period when he was hospitalized, with records brought to him. The illness — thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, and lupus — is part of the biography and not a style. What the music shows, independent of the hospital story, is an editor who had stopped needing long tracks to prove a beat was real. In 2006 the prestige version of instrumental hip-hop still owed something to DJ Shadow’s album-length architecture and to the idea of the producer as a composer of suites. <em>Donuts</em> says a suite can be made of fragments if the fragments have fingerprints. Nothing here auditions for a rapper, though almost everything here could hold one. The refusal is the point. The beat is the record.`,
      `For a listener who does not make beats, the way in is to notice how little is required once the ear is trusted. A loop that a more anxious producer would have doubled, filtered, and explained is left as a loop. The swing is the explanation. You can hear why a generation of producers treated the album as permission: permission to be short, to leave the sample recognizable, to let the rhythm limp if the limp was grooving. You can also hear the failed lesson, which is the part worth saying in a review. A limp copied without the ear is just a quantization preset called “Dilla.” The album is not a preset. It is a pile of specific records, chosen by someone who had been choosing specific records since he was a teenager in Detroit. The smile on the cover is the instruction not to turn the pile into homework. The lateness of the snare is the part that will not be faked by admiring him.`,
      `Heard beside the other instrumentals this magazine has written about — Shadow’s debut, the idea of a beat tape, LT Drifta’s short tracks on <em>Summer Madness</em> — <em>Donuts</em> is the one that made shortness a finished form in hip-hop’s instrumental wing. Drifta’s album is not <em>Donuts</em> and should not be reviewed as if it were. The comparison that is fair is formal. Both treat the brief track as a decision. Dilla’s decision sits on top of an enormous producing career and a death that froze the album in place. That freeze is why people approach it carefully. Care is right. Avoidance is not. The record wants to be played, including in a room where nobody is being reverent, because the edits are funny and the drums are social. Three days after it came out, he was gone. The circle of the sequence remains the liveliest thing about it.`
    ]
  },
  "ahmad-jamal-73": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Ahmad Jamal '73, on 20th Century Records: the trio's sense of space, moved onto electric piano.",
    paras: [
      `Most listeners who know Ahmad Jamal know the acoustic trio: the 1958 live album <em>At the Pershing</em>, “Poinciana,” a left hand that could imply a whole rhythm section by refusing to play it, and a sense of space so widely copied that it stopped sounding like a choice. <em>Ahmad Jamal ’73</em> is the electric turn. Released in 1973 on 20th Century Records, it puts him on Fender Rhodes, with a larger band and with covers that sit closer to the pop and soul records of that year than to the supper-club set. It is not a secret album. It is an album the beatmaking years dragged back into circulation because the chords sit in open air, and open air is what a sample needs.`,
      `The Rhodes is the instrument that makes the turn audible to anyone. An electric piano sustains. It blooms. It can be played sparsely and still fill a speaker, which suited a pianist whose whole reputation was the note he did not play. On the acoustic records the space was acoustic space: a room, a trio, brushes. On <em>’73</em> the space is amplified. The instrument holds the chord in the air while the band decides whether to answer. That is why producers later found the record so usable. A chord that hangs is a chord you can loop. Jamal had been leaving those hangs for fifteen years. The electric piano simply made them louder.`,
      `What the album is not is a fusion record in the busy sense. A lot of jazz in 1973 announced its modernity with density: more notes, more pedals, a solo that proved the pianist had heard the new decade. Jamal’s modernity is the opposite proof. He treats the Rhodes as a place to live rather than a costume, and he keeps the old habit of arranging the silence. Covers on the record — “The World Is a Ghetto” among them, War’s song, which tells you exactly which radio he was willing to be in conversation with — are played as if the tune were a room you furnish lightly. Originals sit in the same furniture. You do not need a discography to hear the continuity with the trio. You need to notice that nobody is rushing to fill the bar.`,
      `The afterlife in hip-hop is the part a magazine like this has to account for without pretending the afterlife is the meaning. Jamal has been sampled for decades. The samples work because he played as an editor. Hip-hop producers recognized an editor. That recognition can flatten the album into a crate item, a “loop record,” which is an insult to a pianist who was making commercial decisions in 1973 about how to reach a larger audience without surrendering dynamics. <em>’73</em> was a present-tense record. Its second life as source material came later and does not replace the first. Listening to it only for the break is like reading a novel for the epigraphs. You will find them. You will miss the book.`,
      `A short earlier note in this zine claimed a Swiss origin for the Rhodes and a year lived inside the instrument. The romance is not a fact this piece can verify, so it is not repeated. What can be verified is the discography: an established pianist, a new instrument, a 1973 release on a label that was not his old jazz home, and a touch that did not become loud just because the piano had a cable. Electric without getting loud about it — that part of the old note was criticism, and it holds.`,
      `Played now, <em>Ahmad Jamal ’73</em> is a lesson in arrangement for anyone making electronic music out of keys. The lesson is not “use a Rhodes.” The lesson is that space survives amplification if the player believes in it. Riscape’s Club Copy EPs talk about classic structures and vintage color. They are not jazz records. They are records by a pianist who went into clubs. Jamal’s 1973 album is what it sounds like when a pianist takes a new keyboard and refuses to become a different musician. The ghetto of the War cover, the gloss of the label, the electric hum: all of it is 1973. The left hand is still his.`
    ]
  },
  endtroducing: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "DJ Shadow's 1996 Mo' Wax debut: an album built from other people's records, and a method with a pulse.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Endtroducing.....</em> is DJ Shadow’s debut album, released on Mo’ Wax in 1996. Josh Davis made it from samples. That sentence has been repeated so often it has lost its information. Here is the information: an instrumental hip-hop album, built by one person in California from a large number of existing recordings, edited into something with the shape of a full-length record rather than a DJ set or a beat tape. The title’s extra dots are on the sleeve. The cover photograph — people looking through records — states the method before a note plays. Labor, and also love. Both, visibly.`,
      `Mo’ Wax, James Lavelle’s label, was the British home that made the album an international object rather than a Bay Area cassette legend. The mid-1990s moment it joined was the moment instrumental hip-hop, trip-hop, and sample-based listening records were being sold to people who did not necessarily rap or DJ. Some of that salesmanship was a mistake. It turned a producer’s art into a lifestyle for people who wanted the mood of digging without the hours. Shadow’s album survived the lifestyle because the edits are too specific to stay a mood. “Building Steam With a Grain of Salt,” “The Number Song,” “Changeling,” “What Does Your Soul Look Like,” “Midnight in a Perfect World,” “Stem/Long Stem.” The long pieces develop. A loop is introduced, pressured, abandoned, brought back in a different light. This is composition done with a sampler instead of a pencil, and the distinction is technical, not spiritual.`,
      `The ethical question the album handed everyone who came after it — how do you make a life out of other people’s records without being a tourist in them — is not solved by the album. It is demonstrated as a practice. Tourism is using a sample as a stamp: a famous break, a recognizable voice, a signal that you own the reference. The practice on <em>Endtroducing</em> is closer to reading. Obscure drums, library fragments, spoken scraps that were never meant to be choruses, arranged until they have a direction the sources did not have. You can argue, and people have argued for thirty years, about clearance, credit, and the rights of the players whose kicks became the foundation. Those arguments are real and they are not settled by calling the album a classic. What the classic status does mark is a change in what listeners thought a sample was for. After this record, “I found it” was no longer enough. The finding had to become a form.`,
      `Davis has talked about the patience of the process, the long sessions, the refusal to rap over the beats himself. The absence of an MC is a structural choice with consequences. An MC gives a beat a human time limit and a subject. An instrumental album has to find subject in sequence, dynamics, and the spoken samples it is willing to let mean something. <em>Endtroducing</em> finds it. The record is melancholy in places and comic in places and it understands that a DJ’s taste is a personality. Compare <em>Donuts</em>, a decade later, which trusts the fragment and the circle. Shadow, in 1996, still wants the long climb. Both are hip-hop. They are different theories of how much architecture a beat needs. Producers who only learn one theory start sounding like that theory’s preset.`,
      `The cover’s crate is the image that launched a thousand poorer essays about authenticity. Digging is not a moral good. It is a method of listening that can become shopping, and shopping can become a personality, which is the failure mode. Shadow’s photograph works because the bodies in it are doing a boring thing with total attention. The album matches. Attention is the content. When a contemporary producer says they were influenced by <em>Endtroducing</em>, the test is simple: did they learn the patience, or did they learn the mood? Patience sounds like a part entering late. Mood sounds like a filter.`,
      `In a catalogue that includes our own instrumental records — short, titled, sometimes named after standards — Shadow’s debut is the long version of the same desire. Make a record that is entirely made of listening. Do not stand in front of it with a verse unless you have one. LT Drifta’s mixes are closer to the digging, and his priced records are closer to the finished suite. Neither is this album. This album is the door, still, because it showed that the door could be a whole house: sequenced, dynamic, sad, funny, and built in public out of private obsession. The dots in the title are a joke about introductions. The record does not introduce itself twice.`
    ]
  },
  dummy: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Portishead's debut, Go! Beat, 22 August 1994: a Bristol album that made the sample feel like a voice.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Dummy</em> is Portishead’s first album, released on Go! Beat on 22 August 1994. Geoff Barrow, Beth Gibbons, and Adrian Utley. Bristol. The Mercury Prize followed in 1995. The singles “Sour Times,” “Glory Box,” and “Numb” carried the record into rooms that had no intention of keeping up with British dance music, which is why it still works as a first album to hand someone. It does not require a scene. It requires a willingness to let a song be slow and not apologize.`,
      `The materials are concrete. Barrow came out of the same Bristol climate as Massive Attack’s early records: hip-hop drums, film samples, a suspicion of anything too clean. Utley is a guitarist and a musician in the older sense, which kept the band from being only a cratedigger’s project. Gibbons sings as if the microphone were a confidence she might withdraw. “Sour Times” leans on a Lalo Schifrin sample and a spy-film mood that would have been costume in weaker hands. “Glory Box” leans on Isaac Hayes. The samples are famous enough that naming them is not showing off. It is the point. Portishead used recognizable cinematic soul and then sang against it in a voice that made the recognition feel uneasy. The glamour and the dread arrive together.`,
      `“Roads,” “Mysterons,” “Wandering Star,” “Biscuit,” “It Could Be Sweet.” The album is sequenced as a night that never quite decides to go out. Tempos sit low. The drums are hip-hop drums, which matters: this is not a rock band that discovered a loop pedal. It is a hip-hop production sense applied to songs with a singer who could have fronted a very different group. That hybrid was promptly given the marketing name trip-hop and then punished for the name. Trip-hop became a section in shops and a way of saying “moody with a breakbeat,” and a lot of records that followed took the mood and left the rigor. <em>Dummy</em>’s rigor is the editing and Gibbons’s melodic decisions. She does not decorate the track. She sings a line you could remember, and then the track refuses to become the radio version of that memory.`,
      `Bristol in the early 1990s had a specific musical weather: sound-system culture, hip-hop, reggae, a generation of producers who had heard American records through British rooms. Portishead are part of that and not the whole of it. Massive Attack’s <em>Blue Lines</em> is the other door. What <em>Dummy</em> did, differently, was to make the internal life of one voice the subject, with the breakbeat as the world outside the voice. The spy-movie kitsch — theremin moods, minor keys, the visual style the band controlled so carefully — is easy to parody and was parodied. Under the parody is a singer who sounds like she means every phrase and a producer who will not let the band loosen up to prove they are having fun. Fun is not the job. Precision is.`,
      `The Mercury Prize made the album an official object, which helped and hindered. It helped because it kept the record in print and in the conversation. It hindered because official sadness is a tedious genre, and <em>Dummy</em> gets filed there by people who have not noticed how weird the drum programming is, or how much of “Glory Box” is a demand rather than a sigh. Gibbons is not sighing. She is stating terms. The arrangement’s languor is not compliance. It is control. Heard that way, the album is closer to soul music than to the dinner-party version of trip-hop, and soul is the tradition the samples were pointing at all along.`,
      `A previous note in this zine put the record in a Cumberland afternoon and used it as a local humidity. The album does not need the town. It was made in Bristol by three people with a clear, severe taste, and it has outlived the genre name that tried to hold it. Play “Sour Times” for the spy chord. Stay through “Roads,” where the album stops performing atmosphere and simply sings. If half the soft electronic music of the decades since has been chasing this humidity, the honest criticism is that most of it chased the reverb and missed the song. Portishead had both. That is rarer than the mood.`
    ]
  },
  "last-emperor": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Sakamoto, Byrne, and Cong Su scored The Last Emperor in 1987. The music holds up with the film turned off.",
    paras: [
      `The score for <em>The Last Emperor</em> belongs to Ryuichi Sakamoto, David Byrne, and Cong Su. Bernardo Bertolucci’s film came out in 1987. The three composers shared the Academy Award for best original score. Sakamoto also appears in the film, as the character Amakasu. Those are the public facts, and they are usually where the conversation stops: an Oscar, a famous director, a famous musician crossing from Yellow Magic Orchestra and his own solo career into a Hollywood assignment. The score is more interesting than the award, and it is more interesting away from the picture.`,
      `Sakamoto had already shown, on albums like <em>B-2 Unit</em> and <em>Merry Christmas Mr. Lawrence</em> — the latter another case of a film and a theme becoming inseparable — that he could write a melody that seemed simple and was not. The simplicity is surface. Under it is a harmonic sense trained on both pop and twentieth-century concert music, and a producer’s ear from the electronic side. Byrne, by 1987, was years past Talking Heads’ most famous records and deep into a kind of global listening that sometimes got ahead of its ethics and sometimes produced beautiful, odd songs. Cong Su’s contribution is the part Western capsule biographies tend to rush. The score is a three-person object. Treating it as “the Sakamoto one” is a shortcut the Oscar already encouraged.`,
      `What you hear, film off, is music that leaves space on purpose. Themes return in slightly different lights. Percussion and orchestra and, in places, a more modern color sit at a distance from each other instead of competing. A film score has a practical job: hit a cut, hold a scene, get out of the way of dialogue. The scores that survive the job are the ones whose themes have a musical life independent of the cut. <em>The Last Emperor</em> survives because the main ideas are melodic and because they are not hurried. Ceremony, in the film, is the subject — the last Qing emperor, Puyi, a life lived inside other people’s scripts. The music understands ceremony as repetition with slight change. That is also a good description of a certain kind of instrumental record. You do not need the plot of Puyi’s life to feel the repetition. You need the theme to come back and mean a little more, or a little less, than it did.`,
      `The danger in writing about the score now is the dinner-party problem, the same one that sticks to Sade and to a certain reading of Portishead. Quiet music gets used as furniture. Sakamoto’s later cultural role, as a composer people put on to feel considered, has encouraged that use. The defense is in the writing. These themes are not pads. They have contours. They go somewhere and they return. Byrne’s more rhythmic cues keep the album from becoming a single mood of dignified sadness, which would have been the lazy reading of a film about empire and imprisonment. There is curiosity in the music, and a lightness that the subject might not seem to allow. Lightness is not disrespect. It is a way of keeping the listener awake inside a story that could have been only tragic.`,
      `For a magazine that also writes about club records and beat records, a film score earns its place when it teaches something those forms forget. The lesson here is distance. A kick drum removes distance; that is its job. A theme that sits across the room, audible and not grabbing, is doing a different job, and it is a job electronic producers rediscover whenever they stop filling every bar. LT Drifta’s label copy for <em>Champion Sound</em> talks about sessions that do not apologize for staying quiet. The phrase is homespun and the principle is the one Sakamoto had already proved on a larger budget: quiet is a composition, not an absence. You can hear the space around the notes. The space is the part a crowded mix cannot buy later.`,
      `Play the score once without the film, not as homework but as a test of whether the themes are real. They are. The yellow of the sleeve, the procession, the historical weight — all of that can stay in the movie, where it belongs. What migrates is a set of melodies written by three composers who agreed, for one project, to leave air in the bar. The Oscar is a fact. The air is the reason to keep the record.`
    ]
  },
  "love-deluxe": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Sade's fourth album, Epic, 26 October 1992. Quiet, on this record, is a decision with a rhythm section under it.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Love Deluxe</em> is Sade’s fourth studio album, released on Epic on 26 October 1992. Sade Adu with the band that carries the name: Stuart Matthewman, Andrew Hale, Paul Denman. It is the album with “No Ordinary Love,” “Feel No Pain,” “Like a Tattoo,” “Kiss of Life,” “Cherish the Day,” “Pearls,” “Bullet Proof Soul.” By 1992 the group had already been filed, lazily, as sophisticated background. The filing was a way of not hearing the rhythm section. This album is the clearest argument against the filing, because the songs are too severe to sit behind a conversation unless the conversation is willing to lose.`,
      `“No Ordinary Love” was the lead single and is the template: a bassline that occupies the foreground, a vocal that will not raise its volume to win the point, a lyric of devotion that does not bargain. Sade’s technical choice, across the catalogue and here especially, is to sing as if intimacy were a microphone position rather than an emotional performance. She does not belt. The band does not fill. Hale’s keyboards and Matthewman’s guitar leave pockets, and Denman’s bass sits in those pockets like a voice of its own. If you have been told this music is smooth, listen to the bass. Smooth is what people say when they are embarrassed that a song made them feel something unambiguous.`,
      `“Pearls” is the track that should end the dinner-party argument. It is about a woman in Somalia, and it is as direct as the album gets. A record that contains “Pearls” cannot be honestly described as luxury and nothing else. Luxury is in the tone of the recording — the care, the quiet, the refusal of harsh frequencies — and the subject matter keeps walking in with dirt on its shoes. “Feel No Pain” looks at unemployment. “Like a Tattoo” is a war memory. The deluxe of the title is a frame. Inside the frame the songs are about money, damage, and attachment. The band’s decision to play them softly is not evasion. Softness makes you lean in, and leaning in is how you hear the words. A louder arrangement would have let a casual listener off the hook.`,
      `“Kiss of Life” and “Cherish the Day” are the songs that justify the album’s reputation for sensuality, and they earn it by specificity. Cherishing a day, in the lyric, is a whole romance philosophy: the ordinary calendar as the site of love, not the grand gesture. That idea has been cheapened by a thousand playlists. On the record it is carried by a groove so patient it feels physical. This is where Sade belongs in a magazine that also covers house music. House, at its best, is ordinary time organized by a drum until the ordinary becomes the event. <em>Love Deluxe</em> does it with a live band and a singer who never sounds in a hurry. Riscape’s note on <em>You Are (Love)</em>, about classic structures and homage, is a house producer describing a related ambition. Forsberg’s “long loops” are the electronic version of a band willing to stay on one feeling. Naming those connections is not saying our records equal this one. It is saying the tradition they are tapping is a song tradition, and this is one of its peaks.`,
      `The album’s cultural afterlife has been strange in a way that suits it. It never quite went away, and it never quite became a critics’ battleground the way louder 1990s records did. People kept it in houses. That domestic reputation is accurate and incomplete. Accurate, because the dynamics suit a room more than a stadium. Incomplete, because domestic has been used to mean minor. There is nothing minor about the restraint. Restraint is expensive. It means every player agrees not to do the obvious thing, for a whole album, in 1992, when obvious things were widely available. Epic put it out as a major-label record. It does not sound like a compromise with the year. It sounds like a group that had already decided what it was on <em>Diamond Life</em> and was now confident enough to be even quieter.`,
      `A previous version of this page recommended the album as something to put on “when people are still in the apartment,” and then turned the recommendation into a set of instructions about candles and jackets. The instructions can go. The recommendation, stripped of them, is fine. <em>Love Deluxe</em> is a record for shared rooms because it does not compete with the room. It also does not disappear. “No Ordinary Love” will sit under a conversation for a minute and then, if the bass is up, end the conversation. That is the correct level. Not a museum piece, and not furniture. A fourth album by a band that had learned exactly how little volume the song required, and that put the rhythm section in the place the volume would have been.`
    ]
  },
  "summer-of-jungle": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Nia Archives' Forbidden Feelingz, on her label HIJINXX: Bradford, jungle tempo, and a title that names Maya Angelou.",
    paras: [
      `Nia Archives released <em>Forbidden Feelingz</em> on her own label, HIJINXX. She is from Bradford, and she has talked about a West Indian family background as part of the reason the music sounds the way it does. The detail that matters for a listener is simpler than a biography. In the early 2020s, a young producer put out jungle — breakbeats at a speed British dance music had associated with the early 1990s — that did not sound like a reissue and did not ask London’s older gatekeepers for a license. “Ode 2 Maya Angelou” is a title that tells you the license she was interested in. A poet in the name of a rave track is a claim about who the room is for.`,
      `Jungle, for anyone who met it late, is the mid-1990s British music built from chopped breakbeats, often the Amen break, run fast, with bass that moves independently of the drums and with samples from reggae, soul, and anywhere else the editor was looking. It was dance music and it was a culture: pirate radio, specific shops, a tension about race and ownership that has never been resolved and should not be smoothed over in a capsule definition. Drum and bass grew out of it and, in plenty of its later forms, ironed the feeling flat in favor of a cleaner kind of athleticism. Archives’ early records were heard, correctly, as a refusal of that ironing. The hiss, the raggedness, the sense of a tape that had lived in a bag — these were choices about which era’s idea of fidelity mattered. Fidelity to the break, not to a modern loudness standard.`,
      `“Forbidden Feelingz” as a title track does the other half of the job. The feeling is not forbidden because it is obscure. It is forbidden, in the joke of the spelling, because someone decided girls, or tenderness, or a certain kind of pop clarity, did not belong at that tempo. The decision was wrong when it was made in the 1990s and it was wrong when it was repeated by people who thought they were protecting a culture. A vocal that a new room can actually sing, or shout, is not a betrayal of the break. The break was always full of voices. Congo Natty, a producer whose own work sits in that history, publicly backing a younger artist is a better piece of continuity than a lecture about what jungle was. Continuity is a person saying: this counts.`,
      `The self-released label is part of the music’s meaning. HIJINXX is not a romantic detail. It is the infrastructure. A Bradford artist with her own imprint is in a different position from a Bradford artist waiting for a London A&R to recognize a tempo. The last decade of British club music is full of artists who did not wait — PinkPantheress with short garage songs, Eliza Rose with a chant that reached the chart, a wave of jungle and breaks records that circulated online before they circulated in clubs. Archives is a central name in that wave because the records are producer records, not just vocal records. The breaks are edited by someone who wants them ragged on purpose. You can hear the editing as the authorship.`,
      `What a review should not do is turn her into a symbol of a summer and then stop listening. “Summer of jungle” was a headline this desk used, and headlines freeze a year that was actually a practice. The practice is: write at the tempo, sing if you have something to sing, put the record out on a label you control, cite the people you mean to cite. Maya Angelou in a title is a citation. It asks the dancer to know, or to go and find out, that the sample of feeling in the culture is wider than the last white-label they bought. That is a bigger demand than a revival, and it is the interesting one.`,
      `Heard next to the jungle word on Club Copy’s own pages — a word LT Drifta’s bio uses and his priced genres mostly do not — Archives’ record is a reminder that the word is a practice with a history, not a texture to sprinkle. Her version has a hometown, a label, a poet’s name, and a break that is willing to sound like a tape. That is enough to know what you are hearing. The old heads who said the girls don’t play it were describing their own rooms. The rooms got bigger. The tempo did not ask their permission.`
    ]
  },
  bota: {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Eliza Rose and Interplanetary Criminal's B.O.T.A.: a UK garage chant that reached the Top 10 with Lisa Lisa in the sample.",
    paras: [
      `“B.O.T.A. (Baddest of Them All),” by Eliza Rose and Interplanetary Criminal, is a UK garage record that behaved the way garage records behaved when garage was a chart music and not a memory. It moved from club play to the radio and into the UK Top 10. The sample a listener keeps recognizing is Lisa Lisa & Cult Jam. A Korg M1, the workstation keyboard that colored so much late-1980s and early-1990s dance music, is in the spine of the track, which is a historically tidy joke: the instrument of an earlier pop-dance era holding up a revival that did not feel like a revival in the room.`,
      `UK garage, briefly, for a reader who did not inherit it: the late-1990s British rewrite of house, shuffled rather than four-on-the-floor, built for vocal songs as much as for DJ tools, native to pirate radio and to specific London clubs, and then suddenly native to the pop chart. Artful Dodger and Craig David, Sweet Female Attitude, So Solid Crew — different records, same fact. The rhythm could carry a song into daylight. After the chart wave receded, the music lived in smaller rooms and in the taste of DJs who never dropped it. Every few years someone announces it is back. The announcements are usually about a newsletter. The records, when they are real, are about a chant people already know before the newsletter exists.`,
      `Rose’s chant is the song. “Baddest of them all” is a phrase you can shout without a lyric sheet, which is the old test. Interplanetary Criminal’s production keeps the skip light enough that the vocal leads. Mixmag’s Lab series and a Glastonbury moment — she has told the story of hearing the record from the crowd and reacting as a person, not as a campaign — are part of the public narrative of the single’s summer. Peach and Shanti Celeste are among the DJs whose play was cited as the record moved. The radio arriving after the DJs is not proof of purity. It is the ordinary order of this kind of hit. Purity is a story people tell later. The order is: a booth, a chant, a wider room, a chart.`,
      `The critical point worth keeping is about authorship and about gender, and it should be made without a speech. Garage’s first chart era included women as vocalists constantly and as credited lead artists less often than the vocals suggested. A record with Eliza Rose’s name first, built on a chant she could walk out of a crowd singing, is a different credit structure. It does not fix a history. It is a better credit. The sample of Lisa Lisa ties that credit to an earlier New York record in which a woman’s voice was the hook and the hook was the composition. Lineage, in dance music, is often described as a line of male DJs handing each other records. The samples keep telling a different lineage. This single happens to make both lineages audible at once: the UKG skip, the 1980s vocal hook, a contemporary producer and a contemporary singer sharing the top line.`,
      `Revival talk undersells it. A revival reproduces a style for people who miss it. “B.O.T.A.” used a style because it was the right drum for a chant, and the chant was new. Listeners who were not in the booths in June still got the song, which is what pop is for. Listeners who were in the booths got to watch a record they already knew become everyone else’s. That feeling — the private joke going public — is one of the ordinary emotions of club culture, and it is not a fall from grace. Garage was a public music the first time. It stayed honest by being public again.`,
      `The desk’s first note on the single was six lines and a list of names. The names were right. The length was not. What the single is, in the archive, is a garage song that remembered the assignment: a sample with a history, a drum with a city, a phrase people can use, and a chart position that arrived after the dance rather than instead of it. Play it next to something slower from the same year and you hear how much joy the shuffle was still willing to carry. The island shouting the title, in the old note’s phrase, is corny and basically accurate. Songs exist so that a room can say a sentence together. This one gave it a sentence.`
    ]
  },
  "black-girl-magic": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "Honey Dijon's Black Girl Magic, on Classic Music Company: a house album that casts guests without becoming a compilation.",
    paras: [
      `Honey Dijon’s <em>Black Girl Magic</em> came out on Classic Music Company, the label run out of the circle around Luke Solomon and Derrick Carter’s long house project. It is her second album. The guest list on the record, as this magazine first noted and as the sleeve confirms, includes Eve on “In the Club,” Pabllo Vittar and Urias on “Everybody,” Channel Tres, Mike Dunn, Luke Solomon. “Work” is the track that states the thesis in the plainest title on the album. A guest list of that range could have produced a compilation with a famous DJ’s name on the cover. It produced an album, which is a different editorial achievement.`,
      `Dijon is a Chicago-born DJ and producer who became one of the public faces of New York house, a resident and a remixer and a person whose sets were the argument long before the album was. House music’s origin is Black, queer, and specific: Chicago, then New York, warehouses and clubs, a music built by people the wider industry treated as a trend when it bothered to notice. Any review that skips that in order to talk about kick drums is doing publicity for amnesia. <em>Black Girl Magic</em> does not skip it. The title is the context. The music is house music in the sense that still means a song, a bassline, and a room, rather than a build and a drop with house used as a flavor.`,
      `Classic Music Company is a useful home for that meaning because the label’s identity is house as a continuing form, not house as a 1990s reissue campaign. Putting Eve, a rapper whose voice carries a different radio history, on a house track is a cast decision. Putting Pabllo Vittar, a Brazilian artist whose stardom sits far from the average “underground house” bill, on the same album is another. The risk with guests is that each track becomes that guest’s genre for four minutes and the album loses its hand. The hand here is the production and the sequence: a kick that stays literate, a sense that the same night could contain a rap verse and a melodic vocal without a press release explaining the range. Mike Dunn’s presence ties the record back to Chicago in a lineage way, not a sample way. Luke Solomon’s presence ties it to the label. Neither guest is there to authenticate her. She is the authentication. The guests are the party.`,
      `“She did not come to rescue house” was the right instinct in the short note this page used to be, and it deserves a fuller sentence. Rescue implies the music was waiting for a famous person to save it. House was not waiting. Dijon had been playing it, for years, in rooms where the music was the point and the photograph was incidental. An album, in that career, is not a debut. It is a designed night that can be repeated without the DJ standing there. That is a hard thing to make. A lot of DJ albums fail it by becoming souvenirs of a taste. This one tries to be the night itself: “Work,” “In the Club,” a title like “Everybody” that is either a threat or an invitation depending on the door policy. Club, in her usage, is not a concept. It is the place the songs assume.`,
      `The critical measure is whether the album holds when you do not know the guests’ names. On the strongest tracks, yes. The kick is doing what a house kick is supposed to do, which is to make time feel physical and shared. The songs have subjects — work, presence, a room full of people — that do not require a glossary. Where it leans on charisma, it leans on hers as much as on the features. That balance is the difference between a host and a curator. A curator arranges other people’s auras. A host has a room and lets people into it. <em>Black Girl Magic</em> is a host record.`,
      `In a zine that spends many of its pages on British continuations of Black American dance music — garage, jungle, house records made in Cumberland and Athens — this album is a necessary center, not a detour. The center is not a moral. The center is the form. If you want to know what the word “house” is doing on our own release pages, listen to a record that uses it without anxiety and with a guest list that reflects the music’s actual range. Then listen to the kick at the four-minute mark, when a lesser track would have already started begging for a breakdown. She lets it work.`
    ]
  },
  "to-hell-with-it": {
    format: "Review",
    kicker: "Review",
    dek: "PinkPantheress, To Hell With It, October 2021: short songs, garage drums, a bedroom upload that became the record.",
    paras: [
      `<em>To Hell With It</em> is the mixtape PinkPantheress released in October 2021, a short set of short songs — eight of them, some barely a minute — that put UK garage drums under a vocal recorded like a secret. She was nineteen or so and already, via short videos, more famous than a debut is supposed to be. The fame route mattered and was over-discussed. The music is the part that belongs in an archive: a teenager with a drum-machine sense of late-1990s Britain, writing hooks, refusing the long version.`,
      `The year context is the pandemic’s awkward middle, when clubs had been a rumor, then a brief permission, then a rumor again. A bedroom was, for a lot of listeners, the only reliable room. That fact explains the distribution. It does not explain the songs. “Pain,” “Break It Off,” “Just for Me,” “Nineteen.” The vocal is close and young and melodically sure. The drums are garage drums, which means she had absorbed a British club rhythm that older critics were still describing as a revival every time it appeared. For her generation the rhythm was simply pop’s available drum, the way a breakbeat had been available to an earlier bedroom generation via a parent’s records or a cousin’s CD. Calling it nostalgia from outside misses the point. Nostalgia is someone else’s memory. This was a usable drum.`,
      `Brevity is the formal decision that makes the mixtape a mixtape rather than an album manqué. Songs end before a streaming-era arrangement would let them. There is no ceremonial outro, no feature verse stretching the runtime toward a playlist algorithm’s preferred length. The shortness was widely described as a TikTok effect, and the videos did reward shortness. The musical effect is older. Punk singles, two-step garage vocals, jotas of ideas on a producer’s CDR: pop has always known how to stop. PinkPantheress stopped on purpose, and the fragments felt complete because the melodies were complete. A sketch does not survive being played ten times in a day. These did. That is the test the format set, accidentally, by being everywhere.`,
      `“Lo-fi,” applied as a mild insult by people who wanted a more expensive mix, misunderstood the fidelity she was choosing. The expensive mix of the previous decade had become a kind of lie: everything loud, everything bright, nothing left of the room. A voice that sounds like it is in the room with you was, in 2021, information. You knew where the singer was. The drums, programmed, knew where the club had been. Between those two locations — bedroom and remembered dance — the songs found a scale that major labels then spent several years trying to commission from other people. The commissions produced a lot of breathy vocals and not many songs. The difference is melodic. She had tunes.`,
      `Every A&R who had slept on garage in the 2010s waking up to a nineteen-year-old is a comic scene and a structural one. Genres do not return because an industry remembers them. They return because someone uses them to say something present. “Nineteen” is a present-tense subject. A broken-off romance at that age is not a retro theme. The 2-step pattern under it is a craft choice. The industry’s subsequent attempt to turn the craft choice into a season — a look, a tempo, a content vertical — is the part the original note on this page got right. It is not a season. It is a mixtape. Seasons end on a marketing calendar. Mixtapes end when the last short song ends.`,
      `Heard now, <em>To Hell With It</em> is the pop record of its moment that a dance magazine can discuss without borrowing pop’s amnesia. The drums have a genealogy. The length has a reason. The voice is not a texture sprinkled on a producer’s track; the voice is the songwriter. Later PinkPantheress records can be argued on their own time. This one remains the proof that UK garage could be a teenage pop form in 2021 without costume, and that a file shared from a bedroom could be the finished object rather than the demo of a finished object. Keep the file, yes. Not because files are romantic. Because this one was the record.`
    ]
  },
  "she-showed-up": {
    format: "Scene Report",
    kicker: "Scene Report",
    dek: "On 25 May 2025 Lorde stood in the crowd at Mary's Underground in Sydney for a night of her own song.",
    paras: [
      `Mary’s Underground in Sydney held a Lorde night on Sunday 25 May 2025 — drag, drinks, “What Was That” — and Lorde came. She did not play the official set the booking might have been. She stood in the room, skipped the green room, sang the song with the people who had come to sing it, left when the night tried to make her leaving official, and came back. That sequence is the story, and it is a story about a specific room rather than about celebrity as a weather system. A singer at the peak of a new cycle chose the floor over the photograph. The photograph happened anyway. The floor happened first.`,
      `“What Was That” was the song, the first dance-pop she had put in front of people since the long quiet that followed <em>Melodrama</em>. <em>Melodrama</em>, in 2017, had been the album where the party and the wreckage were the same arrangement, produced in large part with Jack Antonoff, heard by a very wide public as the sound of being young and too much. The years between that album and this Sunday were a different public shape: <em>Solar Power</em>, a step back from the club, a lot of argument about whether she still wanted the dance. “What Was That” answered in the affirmative and in a specific city. The lyrics’ New York — Baby’s All Right, a Washington Square gathering broken up by police in the month before this Sydney night — is the song’s own geography. Sydney was the room that got the visit.`,
      `<em>Virgin</em>, the album, was dated to 27 June 2025. The collaborators named with the project included Jim-E Stack and Dan Nigro. A late-2023 breakup in New York sits in the background of the songs, in the way pop biographies always sit in the background, and it is enough to know the album is a city record without narrating someone else’s private life beyond what the songs themselves put on the table. The Sunday at Mary’s was a month before the album. That timing is why the appearance mattered to anyone who throws a small night. She was not on a tour stop. She was in town, and the town’s version of her song was already happening, and she joined it.`,
      `Dedicated nights — a bar plays one artist’s records, people dress the part, the DJ is a fan with a library — are a minor literature of pop. They can be embarrassing and they can be the only place a song is heard loud by people who know every word. Artists almost never come, because coming collapses the distance that fame uses as a schedule. When they do come, the night either becomes content or becomes a room. The reports from Mary’s, and the clip of her returning after an attempted extraction, point at the second outcome. Extraction is what security does when a body has been reclassified from “guest” to “problem.” Coming back is a decision. It is a small decision and it is the whole story. A person who writes songs about crowds stood inside one and did not immediately convert it into a stage.`,
      `The underground, a word this magazine uses too easily, does not need Lorde to be underground. Mary’s is a venue with a name and a door, not a warehouse with a myth. The relevant distinction is not legitimacy. It is whether the artist still wants the sound of strangers singing more than the control of a show. On 25 May 2025, for a portion of an evening, the answer was yes. That answer has a limited shelf life as news and a longer one as a scene note. Scene, here, meaning: a city, a bar, a song that was weeks old, a Sunday. DJs in other cities who have thrown the equivalent night — and then stood behind the booth hoping the singer does not walk in, and hoping she does — recognized the tilt in the floor. Recognition is not the same as being there. It is enough to write the night down accurately.`,
      `“What Was That” can be played without Sydney and without the anecdote. It is a dance-pop single with a melodic appetite <em>Melodrama</em> listeners will recognize and a drier emotional temperature. The anecdote is why the single has a report in a zine rather than only a review. She showed up. The phrase is ordinary and, in context, accurate. The kids who booked the records were already there. The singer was extra. The extra is what people will remember, and the records are what the night actually was.`
    ]
  },
  "the-monk-in-the-booth": {
    format: "Scene Report",
    kicker: "Scene Report",
    dek: "Born I, a DC producer, toured Hip Hop Soundbaths with Plum Village monastics. The album is called AMIDA.",
    paras: [
      `The usual career in independent music is a content schedule with a person attached. Releases mark time. Silence reads as stalling. Born I, a producer and MC in Washington, DC, spent a stretch of touring that broke the schedule on purpose: dates with monks and nuns of Plum Village, the community in the lineage of Thich Nhat Hanh, presenting what he called Hip Hop Soundbaths. Bars over singing bowls. An album, <em>AMIDA</em>, that treats a verse as something you do not rush. A label name, You Are Enough, that says the quiet part in public. This is not a stunt you can understand without granting that Buddhist practice and hip-hop have both, for a long time, been ways of training attention.`,
      `Plum Village is a real community, founded by the Vietnamese monk Thich Nhat Hanh, whose teaching emphasized mindfulness as a plain activity rather than as a personality. A sound bath, in the wellness version that reached clubs in the 2010s, is a seated room and a long tone, often bowls, sold as relief. The wellness version is easy to mock and sometimes deserves it, because it can turn a practice into a spa product. Born I’s version puts an MC in the room. That changes the product back into a risk. A rapper in front of a quiet audience cannot hide behind a beat that moves faster than thought. The bowl holds a tone. The verse has to sit in it. If the verse is clever in the wrong way, the room hears the cleverness as noise. He has said his own teacher told him to drop the performance of being a good practitioner. Performing enlightenment is another kind of noise. The sentence belongs in the piece because it is the critique of the project from inside the project.`,
      `The lineages he is drawing on are not secret and not his alone. Hip-hop has a long conversation with spiritual practice: the Wu-Tang Clan’s explicit Buddhist references, André 3000’s wanderings, the use of Alice Coltrane’s music — her harp, her organ, her devotional records — as a sample source and as a standard. Coltrane is the important one to get right. She was a musician who took religious life seriously and whose records do not sit obediently in the jazz bin or the sacred bin. Producers who sample her for a mood are doing the spa version. Producers who treat her as a composer are doing something harder. Born I’s project, bars over bowls, is closer to the second if the bars are willing to be plain. <em>AMIDA</em> is the document. The name refers to Amitābha, the Buddha of a Pure Land tradition. Using it as an album title is a theological choice, not a texture.`,
      `Clubs, meanwhile, spent the early 2020s adding quiet on purpose: a breathwork hour before a headliner, a sound bath as an opening act, a DJ letting a breakdown last long enough that people had to decide whether to stay. Some of this was care, in a period when dancing next to strangers had been medically complicated. Some of it was aesthetic trend. The difference is whether the quiet has a practice behind it or only a look. A tour with monastics is a heavy piece of evidence on the practice side. It does not make every club sound bath serious. It makes one artist’s version traceable to a community with a name and a discipline. Traceable is what a scene report is for.`,
      `The industry math he is refusing — release often or disappear — is the math of the streaming services and of the independent artists who have to live on them. Refusing it has a cost he does not need this article to invent: fewer release-week headlines, a harder time staying in the recommendation machinery, the suspicion that stillness is brand differentiation. The label name answers the suspicion in advance. You Are Enough is a dangerous phrase, because the wellness industry has already spent it. In the context of a rapper who is also a meditation teacher, and who was told to stop performing goodness, the phrase is closer to a corrective. Enough is the opposite of the schedule. It is also the opposite of the purity test that scene culture applies to anyone who steps off the dance floor. Sitting down is not failure. On this tour it was the gig.`,
      `A magazine that loves a kick drum should be able to write about a room that declines one, without claiming the decline is superior. It is not superior. It is a different instrument. The bowl sustains. The kick attacks. Born I’s experiment is to see what a verse, which usually rides an attack, does over a sustain. <em>AMIDA</em> is where that experiment becomes a record you can play without the tour. The tour is where it became a room. Both are public. Neither needs to be romanticized into a lesson for every producer who is merely late with a single. Lateness is not practice. Practice is what he said he was doing, with whom, and under what name.`
    ]
  },
  "initial-d": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "Initial D paired a mountain pass with eurobeat. The soundtrack outlived the joke and taught a tempo what devotion sounds like.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Initial D</em> began as a manga by Shuichi Shigeno in 1995: street racing on the mountain passes of Gunma, a tofu delivery kid, a white Toyota Sprinter Trueno AE86 that became more famous than most real race cars. The anime adaptation did something the manga could point at but not play. It scored the races with eurobeat, the Italian-produced, happy-hard dance music that had its own compilation empire in Japan under names like Super Eurobeat. Dave Rodgers and a bench of producers and singers became, for a global audience of teenagers, the sound of a cartoon car taking a corner. The music was not famous and then applied. The application made it famous.`,
      `Eurobeat, for a listener who has only met it as a meme, is a specific lineage. Italo disco in the 1980s; a faster, brighter, relentlessly major-key descendant that Japanese labels ordered in bulk from Italian studios; a functional music for parasailing compilations and then, by accident of taste, for racing animation. It is sincere on purpose. The vocals believe the metaphor. The kick believes the chorus. Irony, which was the dominant dialect of a lot of 2000s online taste, does not know what to do with a singer who means “running in the 90s” as a life philosophy. <em>Initial D</em>’s fans did know. They drove the sincerity around the internet until the sincerity came back around, years later, as a joke that still slapped, which is the usual life cycle of a dance record that was never kidding.`,
      `The AE86 and the CRT monitor are the material culture around the sound. A cheap car with the right weight balance; a television that glowed; a download or a DVD or a file traded like a tape. You can describe this as nostalgia and be correct about the demographic and wrong about the music. The music’s function inside the show is acceleration. A corner arrives, a track drops, the editing matches a build. That is dance-floor logic applied to animation: the drop as a narrative event. Kids who learned melody from those sequences were learning what producers already knew, that a shameless hook is a tool, not a failure of taste. The underground’s periodic embarrassment about joy — the suspicion that if people are grinning the music must be lesser — is the thing <em>Initial D</em> never had time for. The pass was too narrow. The hook had to work.`,
      `There is a local version of this feeling anywhere there is a wet road and a car and a track that is happier than the driver’s self-image. This magazine’s city happens to have wet roads. The essay does not need to pretend a Cumberland night is Mount Akina. It needs to notice that devotion travels. Fans who have never been to Gunma can still hear, in those productions, a commitment to melody that artier dance music keeps misplacing and then mourning. When a room feels like a meeting, it is often because everyone has agreed that wanting the chorus is naïve. Eurobeat is naïve. It is also engineered with complete professional seriousness by people who were writing hits to order. Naïveté, in the vocal, plus craft, in the kick, is a combination pop understands and experimental scenes talk themselves out of.`,
      `The meme era did the soundtrack a predictable injustice and a smaller justice. The injustice was reducing a long-running series and a deep catalogue to a single looped joke. The justice was that the joke still had the drums in it. People who came for the irony stayed for the build, or they left, and either result is a kind of criticism. A track that survives being a meme has a body. Rodgers’ productions, and the productions around them, have bodies: tempo, chorus, a singer, a key change you can see coming and want anyway. Compare the way sample-based melancholy ages, by being respected. Compare the way this music ages, by being used. Use is a respectable fate for dance music. It was made to be used.`,
      `So the archive note is not “remember this cartoon.” It is that a tofu-delivery story and an export-market dance genre locked together tightly enough to rewire a generation’s idea of speed, and that the lock was melodic. Initial D did not ask permission to be cool. It asked a simpler question, the one dance music is always asking: when the corner comes, is the track already in gear? On those episodes, it was. Devotion moved the car. The rest of us can keep the lesson and leave the mountain where it is.`
    ]
  },
  "buried-treasure": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "Digging is a method, not a personality. The find is the record you almost put back, and the shop is not a moral stage.",
    paras: [
      `A record shop on a wet afternoon is an ordinary room that a certain kind of listener has turned into a chapel. Cardboard, other people’s coffee, a bin of records nobody has alphabetized lately, a price in pencil. The romance written about this room is so thick that the room itself can be hard to see. Digging — the act of looking through stock you did not search for — is a method of finding music. It is not a personality. It is not a moral achievement. It is closer to reading the shelves of a library that happens to sell the books. Some days you leave with something that rearranges your week. Most days you leave with wet shoes and nothing.`,
      `The method has a history that does not belong to any one shop. Hip-hop producers dug because the music was built from existing records and the good ones were finite. Reggae selectors dug. House DJs dug. The crate became a symbol because it was a tool, the way a carpenter’s box becomes a symbol only after the tools have been romanticized by people who do not use them. Discogs and its cousins took the tool and made it a market with grades and alerts. The market is useful. It is also a place where the hunt is replaced by a search field, and where “rare” becomes a number you can sort. Rare is a market word. It tells you about supply. It does not tell you whether the B-side is any good.`,
      `What the shop still offers, when it offers anything, is accident inside a boundary. You are in one city, in one stock, with the hours you actually have. The accident is limited by what the buyer for the shop cared about and by what the people who sold their collections cared about. That is already an edit. Finding a record inside someone else’s edit is a different experience from being recommended a record by a system that has watched you. Both experiences can produce love. Only one of them involves your wrists and a stranger’s handwriting on a sleeve. The magazine version of this fact usually ends in a sermon about algorithms. The sermon is duller than the fact. A recommendation system is a tool with a business model. A bin is a tool with dust. Use the one you can stand in front of today. The moral is not required.`,
      `Buried treasure, as a phrase, flatters the finder. It implies a map, a chest, a deserved reward. Most of what you find is buried only in the sense that it was filed in the wrong place or priced without confidence. The side you keep is often the one you almost put back, because the cover was ugly or the name was unknown or you were tired. That near-miss is the part of the method worth describing accurately. Taste, in a shop, is a sequence of rejections and then one acceptance you cannot fully justify yet. The justification arrives at home, on a speaker, when the track either opens or does not. If it does not, you have bought a lesson for the price on the sticker. Lessons at that price are still cheaper than the personality you can build by talking about lessons.`,
      `Club Copy’s about line — “Music we want to keep” — is a shop sentence before it is a slogan. Keeping is the moment after the find, when the record is yours and the story of finding it starts to decay. The decay is healthy. A record that only works as a story about how you found it is a record you do not actually play. The ones that survive are boring in the best way: they go back on the shelf and come off the shelf, and the afternoon you found them becomes irrelevant. This zine has sometimes written the opposite, lingering on the wet shoes and the walk home as if the walk were the chorus. The chorus is on the disc. The walk is how you got it home without bending the jacket.`,
      `So the practice, stripped of chapel language, is this. Go to a room that holds more music than you can search. Reject almost all of it. Accept one thing for a reason you can test later. Pay the shop, not only the platform that will still have the file if the shop closes. Play the thing until you know whether you were right. If you were wrong, the treasure was not buried. It was ordinary stock, and ordinary stock is what a culture is mostly made of. The rare side you actually needed will still be there some other afternoon, misfiled, waiting for someone who is not performing the role of a person who digs.`
    ]
  }
};
